    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/1010.4009v1</id>
    <title>Cobham's theorem for substitutions</title>
    <updated>2010-10-19T19:10:38Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1010.4009v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/1010.4009v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>The seminal theorem of Cobham has given rise during the last 40 years to a lot of works around non-standard numeration systems and has been extended to many contexts. In this paper, as a result of fifteen years of improvements, we obtain a complete and general version for the so-called substitutive sequences. Let $α$ and $β$ be two multiplicatively independent Perron numbers. Then, a sequence $x\in A^\mathbb{N}$, where $A$ is a finite alphabet, is both $α$-substitutive and $β$-substitutive if and only if $x$ is ultimately periodic.</summary>
